  "grafo_evidencia": {
    "nodes": [
      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "f6f3f9eb-0ab7-5ebf-ad53-9e639951ec99",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants/Genera: Lantana, Silybum, milk thistle\n- Animal Species: Strymon bazochii\n- Compounds/Extracts: Silibinin, silybin, silymarin\n- Concepts: biological control, plant taxonomy\n\nFacts:\n- Lantana is a genus of about 150 species of perennial flowering plants in the verbena family, Verbenaceae.\n- In 1902, Strymon bazochii was introduced to Hawaii to control Lantana species, and this biological control effort proved unsuccessful.\n- Silybum, also called milk thistle, is a genus of two species of thistles in the daisy family.\n- Silibinin, also known as silybin, is named from Silybum, the genus of the plant from which it is extracted.\n- Silibinin is the major active constituent of silymarin, a standardized extract of milk thistle seeds.\n- Silymarin contains a mixture of flavonolignans including silibinin, isosilibinin, silicristin, and silidianin.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "8f0563d0-a0c5-52aa-a0da-8f0082c9910c",
        "text": "Page: Lantana. Sentence: Lantana is a genus of about 150 species of perennial flowering plants in the verbena family, Verbenaceae.\nPage: Silybum. Sentence: Silybum (milk thistle) is a genus of two species of thistles in the (daisy family).\nPage: Strymon bazochii. Sentence: It was introduced to Hawaii in 1902 to control \"Lantana\" species, in which it has proven unsuccessful.\nPage: Silibinin. Sentence: Silibinin (INN), also known as silybin (both from \"Silybum\", the generic name of the plant from which it is extracted), is the major active constituent of silymarin, a standardized extract of the milk thistle seeds, containing a mixture of flavonolignans consisting of silibinin, isosilibinin, silicristin, silidianin, and others.",
